---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import { POSTS } from '../../data/posts';
import { PRICING } from '../../data/brand';
const post = POSTS.find((p) => p.slug === 'free-ecommerce-plans-for-product-testing')!;
---

<PostLayout post={post}>
  <p>
    A free ecommerce plan is the right tool for exactly one job: finding out whether a product sells before you pay
    for a platform. Several tools offer one. They differ in what the free tier includes, how it handles international
    buyers, and where it stops. This article compares four that small cross-border sellers commonly consider, including
    Senvarico, and states each tool's limits plainly. Competitor plan details change; each section links to the
    official site rather than quoting numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>What a testing store actually needs from a free plan</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Enough products for one rotation of tests, usually 10–20 SKUs</li>
    <li>PayPal and card checkout that international buyers trust</li>
    <li>Prices in the buyer's currency</li>
    <li>No platform commission on the few sales a test produces</li>
    <li>A clear, affordable next step when a product works</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Big Cartel</h2>
  <p>
    Big Cartel's free plan is aimed at artists and makers and is limited to a small number of products. It is simple
    and reliable for a creator selling a few items, mostly domestically. Cross-border features are minimal and the
    admin is English only. Details on the <a href="https://www.bigcartel.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">official site</a>.
    See also <a href="/senvarico-vs-big-cartel">Senvarico vs Big Cartel</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Ecwid</h2>
  <p>
    Ecwid's free tier is a store widget you can embed into an existing site, with a low product limit. Its strength is
    selling through several channels at once. The free tier is quite restricted, and the standalone storefront is less
    flexible than a dedicated builder. Details on the <a href="https://www.ecwid.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">official site</a>.
    See also <a href="/senvarico-vs-ecwid">Senvarico vs Ecwid</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Square Online</h2>
  <p>
    Square Online is free to start and is tightly tied to Square payments and point of sale. It suits sellers who
    already sell in person with Square in a country where Square operates. It is not oriented to cross-border
    sellers based elsewhere. Details on the <a href="https://squareup.com/us/en/online-store" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">official site</a>.
    See also <a href="/senvarico-vs-square-online">Senvarico vs Square Online</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Senvarico</h2>
  <p>
    Senvarico's {PRICING.free.name} plan is {PRICING.free.priceLabel} with {PRICING.free.skuLabel.toLowerCase()},
    a {PRICING.platformFee} platform transaction fee, PayPal and Stripe checkout, multi-currency and multi-language
    storefronts, and a Chinese or English admin. It is designed for cross-border sellers testing products for Europe
    and North America, often through TikTok or Facebook ads. When live SKUs pass {PRICING.free.skuLimit}, the next step is the
    {PRICING.paid.name} plan at {PRICING.paid.priceLabel} for {PRICING.paid.skuLabel.toLowerCase()}. There is no separate trial
    because the free plan is permanent, and no annual plan. Senvarico is currently in beta. See <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where each free plan stops</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool</th><th scope="col">Free plan is built for</th><th scope="col">Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Big Cartel</th><td>Creators with a few items, domestic</td><td>Low product cap; weak cross-border; English admin</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Ecwid</th><td>Adding a small store to an existing site</td><td>Very low product cap; limited standalone storefront</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Square Online</th><td>In-person Square sellers going online</td><td>Square-country only; not for overseas cross-border sellers</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Senvarico</th><td>Cross-border product testing, 10–20 SKUs</td><td>{PRICING.free.skuLimit} SKUs; no enterprise features; beta</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to choose</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>If you already have a website: Ecwid.</li>
    <li>If you sell in person with Square in a supported country: Square Online.</li>
    <li>If you are a maker selling a few items at home: Big Cartel.</li>
    <li>If you are testing physical products for Europe and North America with PayPal or Stripe and want a Chinese or English admin: Senvarico.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    None of these is the right long-term platform for a store with hundreds of SKUs. For that stage, see
    <a href="/shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers">Shopify alternatives for small sellers</a>.
  </p>
</PostLayout>
